/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A terminal is a point where an outside wire connects.
   - A one-port has one such pair; a resistor, or any network judged by its input impedance alone, is a one-port.
   - Many two-ports have only three terminals, because terminals 1′ and 2′ are the same wire.
   - To describe a two-port by four fixed constants it must be linear (no powers or products of the port variables), contain no independent sources, and start with zero stored energy in its inductors and capacitors.
   - This book uses the convention found in most textbooks.
   - With both currents entering, the power entering the two-port from outside is
   - where P in watts is positive when the network absorbs power and negative when it delivers power to the outside (a passive network can never give P < 0).
   - A load ZL at port 2 carries the current that leaves terminal 2, which is −I₂.
   - A linear two-port with no internal sources fixes two linear relations among V₁, I₁, V₂ and I₂.
   - Six ways of choosing two variables out of four give six sets.
*/
